4.0 Problem Statement
4.1 Background 
We’ve traveled back in time to the year 2020. Construction is well underway at one of Canada's earliest supertall developments, 1 Bloor Street West, known as The One. Mizrahi Developments submitted a rezoning addendum to the City of Toronto requesting to add more storeys to the approved 85-storey (308.6-metre) design. As an effort to add more luxury residential units to offset rising construction costs and retain the title of Canada's tallest planned building ahead of Pinnacle One Yonge (SkyTower). 
4.2 Scope
Mizrahi Developments has contacted Jacobs. An established engineering consulting firm in Sustainable building design and delivery for resilient buildings that perform better, last longer and adapt to change. Mizrahi Developments has enlisted the help of Jacobs to provide various design solutions for adding additional stories to its existing 85 story design plans. With your experience, you are chosen to lead a team of engineering consultants to propose a new design plan.
4.3 Constraints


You have an original baseline project budget of $1.4 billion (including a $583.2 million fixed-price construction baseline) and you are solely responsible for determining the additional capital budget and time frame needed to complete this vertical expansion. Because construction is already underway in 2020, the 32 rock-socketed bedrock caissons and the lower podium steel mega-columns (Levels 1–9) are already locked in and cannot be excavated or replaced. Your plan must include an evaluation of the economic, environmental, municipal, and social implications of your solution. 
5.0 Evaluation
5.1 Judging
Evaluation will be based on the rubric in Section 5.3 along with fulfilment of the given requirements, solution originality, and persuasiveness of presenters.
The competition organizers will distribute copies of all the reports to all of the judges. The competition organizers will allow sufficient time for the judges to review the reports prior to their respective presentations.
5.2 Delivery
The method of delivery for this competition will be a live presentation and questioning session with the judges.
Presenters may expect brief questions by judges for clarification during their presentation, however detailed questions will be presented after the presentation within the question period.
































